
/*
Problem: Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array
Platform: LeetCode
Difficulty: Easy
Link: https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-duplicates-from-sorted-array/

Approach:
Use two pointers.

Start one pointer from the beginning and another from the second position.

The left pointer `i` keeps track of the position where
the next unique element should be placed.

The right pointer `j` checks every element in the array.

If nums[j] is different from nums[i],
it means we found a new unique element.

Move i forward and copy nums[j] to nums[i].

If nums[j] is equal to nums[i],
it means the element is a duplicate,
so we skip it.

At the end, return i + 1.

Mistake I Made:
I thought I should return i because
I was using 0-based indexing.

But here, the question is asking for the
NUMBER OF UNIQUE ELEMENTS, not an array index.

Therefore, I must return i + 1.

Important Tip:
Always check whether the question is asking for
an INDEX or a COUNT.

In Two Sum II, we returned i + 1 because
the question used 1-based indexing.

But in Remove Duplicates, we return i + 1 because
i is the index of the LAST UNIQUE ELEMENT,
and we need the TOTAL NUMBER OF UNIQUE ELEMENTS.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)
*/

class Solution {
public:
int removeDuplicates(vector<int>& nums) {
int n = nums.size();

```
    int i = 0;

    for (int j = 1; j < n; j++) {
        if (nums[j] != nums[i]) {
            i++;
            nums[i] = nums[j];
        }
    }

    return i + 1;
}
```

};
